**IMPORTANT:** This is a Next.js Pages Router app (NOT react-router-dom). The commented-out code uses `Navigate` and `useLocation` from react-router-dom which are NOT available. 
**Fix:** Since this is Next.js, replace the commented-out react-router-dom approach with Next.js compatible auth:
- Use `useAuth` from `@/contexts/AuthContext` (already imported in many components)
- Use `useRouter` from `next/router` for redirects instead of react-router-dom's Navigate
- Uncomment the permission checking logic but adapt it for Next.js
- Keep the component simple — if auth check fails, show unauthorized message or redirect

Here's the approach:
```typescript
import { ReactNode } from 'react';
import { useRouter } from 'next/router';
import { useAuth } from '@/contexts/AuthContext';
import { Permission, UserRole } from '@/types/auth.types';

export function ProtectedRoute({
  children,
  requireAuth = true,
  requiredPermissions = [],
  requiredRoles = [],
  requireAllPermissions = false,
  fallbackPath = '/login',
  unauthorizedComponent,
}: ProtectedRouteProps) {
  const { currentUser, isAuthenticated, loading, hasAnyPermission, hasAllPermissions, hasAnyRole } = useAuth();
  const router = useRouter();

  if (loading) {
    return <div className="min-h-screen flex items-center justify-center"><p>Loading...</p></div>;
  }

  if (requireAuth && !isAuthenticated) {
    router.replace(fallbackPath);
    return null;
  }

  if (requiredRoles.length > 0 && currentUser) {
    if (!hasAnyRole(requiredRoles)) {
      if (unauthorizedComponent) return <>{unauthorizedComponent}</>;
      return <UnauthorizedMessage />;
    }
  }

  if (requiredPermissions.length > 0 && currentUser) {
    const hasPerms = requireAllPermissions ? hasAllPermissions(requiredPermissions) : hasAnyPermission(requiredPermissions);
    if (!hasPerms) {
      if (unauthorizedComponent) return <>{unauthorizedComponent}</>;
      return <UnauthorizedMessage />;
    }
  }

  return <>{children}</>;
}
```

Also uncomment the UnauthorizedComponent (rename to UnauthorizedMessage for simplicity) but remove the react-router-dom imports and signOut dependency — just show a simple access denied message with a "Go Back" button.

